{"type":"user","uuid":"01a1b6e6-1377-7529-96db-a3e31611b090","session_id":"01a1b6e5-b62d-77fa-b8d6-e09057b99f7b","message":{"content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_00_4UD9S62uwV0ZknjOxuJu3793","content":[{"type":"text","text":"   1→import test from 'node:test'\n   2→import assert from 'node:assert/strict'\n   3→import path from 'node:path'\n   4→import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'\n   5→import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'\n   6→\n   7→import { extractTitles } from '../../scripts/check-traceability.mjs'\n   8→\n   9→const HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\n  10→const ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..', '..')\n  11→const GATE = path.join(ROOT, 'scripts', 'milestone-gate.mjs')\n  12→const DEFECTS = path.join(ROOT, 'scripts', 'check-defects.mjs')\n  13→const FIXTURES = path.join(ROOT, 'test', 'fixtures', 'milestone-gate')\n  14→const M2_PLANNED = path.join(FIXTURES, 'm2-planned')\n  15→const M4_NOCACHE = path.join(FIXTURES, 'm4-nocache')\n  16→\n  17→function runGate(milestone, root) {\n  18→  return spawnSync(process.execPath, [GATE, '--milestone', milestone, '--root', root], { encoding: 'utf8' })\n  19→}\n  20→\n  21→function runDefects(args) {\n  22→  return spawnSync(process.execPath, [DEFECTS, ...args], { encoding: 'utf8' })\n  23→}\n  24→\n  25→function output(result) {\n  26→  return `${result.stdout ?? ''}${result.stderr ?? ''}`\n  27→}\n  28→\n  29→test('[AC-074] m2-planned: M1 passes while M2 is blocked by a planned AC and a missing fix', () => {\n  30→  const m1 = runGate('M1', M2_PLANNED)\n  31→  assert.equal(m1.status, 0, output(m1))\n  32→  assert.match(m1.stdout, /milestone-gate M1: PASS/)\n  33→\n  34→  const m2 = runGate('M2', M2_PLANNED)\n  35→  assert.equal(m2.status, 1, output(m2))\n  36→  assert.match(m2.stdout, /milestone-gate M2: BLOCKED/)\n  37→  assert.match(m2.stdout, /gate\\.ac_not_covered[^\\n]*AC-026/)\n  38→  assert.match(m2.stdout, /defects\\.missing_fixed[^\\n]*a5-shell/)\n  39→  assert.ok(!/gate\\.ac_not_covered[^\\n]*AC-001/.test(m2.stdout))\n  40→})\n  41→\n  42→test('[AC-074] m4-nocache: M3 passes while M4 is blocked only by the missing fix', () => {\n  43→  const m3 = runGate('M3', M4_NOCACHE)\n  44→  assert.equal(m3.status, 0, output(m3))\n  45→  assert.match(m3.stdout, /milestone-gate M3: PASS/)\n  46→\n  47→  const m4 = runGate('M4', M4_NOCACHE)\n  48→  assert.equal(m4.status, 1, output(m4))\n  49→  assert.match(m4.stdout, /milestone-gate M4: BLOCKED/)\n  50→  assert.match(m4.stdout, /defects\\.missing_fixed[^\\n]*a5-nocache/)\n  51→  assert.ok(!/gate\\.ac_not_covered/.test(m4.stdout))\n  52→})\n  53→\n  54→test('[AC-074] check-defects default mode only demands a fix for covered ACs', () => {\n  55→  const planned = runDefects(['--root', M2_PLANNED])\n  56→  assert.equal(planned.status, 0, output(planned))\n  57→  assert.match(planned.stdout, /check-defects: ok/)\n  58→\n  59→  const covered = runDefects(['--root', M4_NOCACHE])\n  60→  assert.equal(covered.status, 1, output(covered))\n  61→  assert.match(covered.stdout, /defects\\.missing_fixed[^\\n]*a5-nocache/)\n  62→})\n  63→\n  64→test('[AC-074] check-defects --milestone demands a fix for every defect up to that milestone', () => {\n  65→  const m1 = runDefects(['--milestone', 'M1', '--root', M2_PLANNED])\n  66→  assert.equal(m1.status, 0, output(m1))\n  67→  assert.ok(!/defects\\.set_mismatch/.test(m1.stdout))\n  68→  assert.ok(!/defects\\.missing_fixed/.test(m1.stdout))\n  69→\n  70→  const m2 = runDefects(['--milestone', 'M2', '--root', M2_PLANNED])\n  71→  assert.equal(m2.status, 1, output(m2))\n  72→  assert.ok(!/defects\\.set_mismatch/.test(m2.stdout))\n  73→  assert.match(m2.stdout, /defects\\.missing_fixed/)\n  74→\n  75→  const m5 = runDefects(['--milestone', 'M5', '--root', M2_PLANNED])\n  76→  assert.equal(m5.status, 1, output(m5))\n  77→  assert.match(m5.stdout, /defects\\.set_mismatch/)\n  78→\n  79→  const invalid = runDefects(['--milestone', 'M9', '--root', M2_PLANNED])\n  80→  assert.equal(invalid.status, 2, output(invalid))\n  81→})\n  82→\n  83→test('[AC-074] the milestone gate refuses to run without a valid milestone', () => {\n  84→  const missing = spawnSync(process.execPath, [GATE, '--root', M2_PLANNED], { encoding: 'utf8' })\n  85→  assert.equal(missing.status, 2, output(missing))\n  86→\n  87→  const invalid = spawnSync(process.execPath, [GATE, '--milestone', 'M9', '--root', M2_PLANNED], {\n  88→    encoding: 'utf8',\n  89→  })\n  90→  assert.equal(invalid.status, 2, output(invalid))\n  91→})\n  92→\n  93→test('[AC-074] extractTitles only counts plain titles for [fixed] and [repro]', () => {\n  94→  const source = [\n  95→    \"test('[repro] broken today', () => {})\",\n  96→    \"test('[fixed] repaired', () => {})\",\n  97→    \"test.skip('[fixed] not a fix', () => {})\",\n  98→    \"test.todo('[fixed] still todo')\",\n  99→  ].join('\\n')\n 100→\n 101→  const titles = extractTitles(source)\n 102→  const plain = titles.filter((item) => item.kind === 'plain').map((item) => item.title)\n 103→  assert.ok(plain.some((title) => title.startsWith('[fixed]')))\n 104→  assert.ok(plain.some((title) => title.startsWith('[repro]')))\n 105→  assert.equal(plain.filter((title) => title.startsWith('[fixed]')).length, 1)\n 106→})\n 107→"}],"is_error":false}],"role":"user"},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b6e5ba867444bd771672006700a3","_messageId":"01a1b6e6077576838d10c6aa595dd0f7","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b6e5ba867444bd771672006700a3","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b6e6077576838d10c6aa595dd0f7","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b6e6077576838d10c6aa595dd0f7","traceparent":"00-4a75c094774db18b4fa9b0e599d0db42-f8dbe608f8e504b0-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b6e5-b62d-77fa-b8d6-e09057b99f7b,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b6e5ba867444bd771672006700a3"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T16:44:54.008Z"}
{"type":"user","uuid":"01a1b6e6-1386-769b-8789-67b73b881068","session_id":"01a1b6e5-b62d-77fa-b8d6-e09057b99f7b","message":{"content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_01_NkJxjTpXtE58mICBitTR6159","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stdout: #!/usr/bin/env node\n// Milestone gate: every AC owned by a task up to the requested milestone must be covered,\n// its defects fixed, and its files must actually run in a full test run.\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nimport os from 'node:os'\nimport path from 'node:path'\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'\n\nimport { mergeShards } from './check-traceability.mjs'\nimport { checkDefects, milestoneRank } from './check-defects.mjs'\n\nconst HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\nconst REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..')\nconst RUN_TESTS = path.join(HERE, 'run-tests.mjs')\n\nfunction collectCoveredFiles(root) {\n  const map = new Map()\n  const dir = path.join(root, 'test', 'traceability')\n  let names = []\n  try {\n    names = fs.readdirSync(dir).filter((name) => name.endsWith('.json'))\n  } catch {\n    return map\n  }\n  for (const name of names) {\n    let shard = null\n    try {\n      shard = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(dir, name), 'utf8'))\n    } catch {\n      continue\n    }\n    for (const entry of Array.isArray(shard?.entries) ? shard.entries : []) {\n      if (!entry || entry.status !== 'covered' || typeof entry.ac !== 'string') continue\n      const files = Array.isArray(entry.files) ? entry.files.filter((file) => typeof file === 'string') : []\n      map.set(entry.ac, files)\n    }\n  }\n  return map\n}\n\nexport function runGate({ root, index, milestone }) {\n  const errors = []\n  const limit = milestoneRank(milestone)\n  const entries = Array.isArray(index?.acs) ? index.acs : []\n\n  const merged = mergeShards({ root, index })\n  for (const error of merged.errors) {\n    const where = error.ac ?? error.file ?? '-'\n    errors.push({ id: 'gate.trace_error', detail: `${where} ${error.detail ?? error.id}`.trim() })\n  }\n\n  for (const entry of entries) {\n    if (!entry || typeof entry !== 'object') continue\n    if (!(milestoneRank(entry.milestone) <= limit)) continue\n    if (merged.acs[entry.ac] !== 'covered') {\n      errors.push({ id: 'gate.ac_not_covered', detail: `${entry.ac} is ${merged.acs[entry.ac] ?? 'planned'}` })\n    }\n  }\n\n  errors.push(...checkDefects({ root, index, milestone }).errors)\n\n  if (errors.length > 0) return errors\n\n  const coveredFiles = collectCoveredFiles(root)\n  const outDir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'cliworker-gate-'))\n  try {\n    const result = spawnSync(process.execPath, [RUN_TESTS, '--root', root, '--out-dir', outDir], {\n      cwd: root,\n      encoding: 'utf8',\n      maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024,\n    })\n    if (result.status !== 0) {\n      const tail = (result.stderr || result.stdout || '').trim().split('\\n').slice(-3).join(' | ')\n      errors.push({ id: 'gate.run_failed', detail: `full run exited ${result.status}${tail ? `: ${tail}` : ''}` })\n      return errors\n    }\n\n    let manifest = null\n    try {\n=== fixtures m2 defects.json ===\n{\r\n  \"defects\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"id\": \"a3-runner\",\r\n      \"owner_task\": \"T-001\",\r\n      \"owner_milestone\": \"M1\",\r\n      \"ac\": \"AC-001\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"id\": \"a5-shell\",\r\n      \"owner_task\": \"T-010\",\r\n      \"owner_milestone\": \"M2\",\r\n      \"ac\": \"AC-026\"\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n\nStderr: (empty)\nExit Code: 0\nSignal: (none)"}],"is_error":false,"_meta":{"rawResponse":{"exitCode":0,"signal":null,"interrupted":false,"sandboxDenied":false,"stderrBytesTruncated":0,"stdoutBytesTruncated":0,"tool_error_code":"0"},"renderer":{"type":"text","value":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// Milestone gate: every AC owned by a task up to the requested milestone must be covered,\n// its defects fixed, and its files must actually run in a full test run.\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nimport os from 'node:os'\nimport path from 'node:path'\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'\n\nimport { mergeShards } from './check-traceability.mjs'\nimport { checkDefects, milestoneRank } from './check-defects.mjs'\n\nconst HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\nconst REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..')\nconst RUN_TESTS = path.join(HERE, 'run-tests.mjs')\n\nfunction collectCoveredFiles(root) {\n  const map = new Map()\n  const dir = path.join(root, 'test', 'traceability')\n  let names = []\n  try {\n    names = fs.readdirSync(dir).filter((name) => name.endsWith('.json'))\n  } catch {\n    return map\n  }\n  for (const name of names) {\n    let shard = null\n    try {\n      shard = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(dir, name), 'utf8'))\n    } catch {\n      continue\n    }\n    for (const entry of Array.isArray(shard?.entries) ? shard.entries : []) {\n      if (!entry || entry.status !== 'covered' || typeof entry.ac !== 'string') continue\n      const files = Array.isArray(entry.files) ? entry.files.filter((file) => typeof file === 'string') : []\n      map.set(entry.ac, files)\n    }\n  }\n  return map\n}\n\nexport function runGate({ root, index, milestone }) {\n  const errors = []\n  const limit = milestoneRank(milestone)\n  const entries = Array.isArray(index?.acs) ? index.acs : []\n\n  const merged = mergeShards({ root, index })\n  for (const error of merged.errors) {\n    const where = error.ac ?? error.file ?? '-'\n    errors.push({ id: 'gate.trace_error', detail: `${where} ${error.detail ?? error.id}`.trim() })\n  }\n\n  for (const entry of entries) {\n    if (!entry || typeof entry !== 'object') continue\n    if (!(milestoneRank(entry.milestone) <= limit)) continue\n    if (merged.acs[entry.ac] !== 'covered') {\n      errors.push({ id: 'gate.ac_not_covered', detail: `${entry.ac} is ${merged.acs[entry.ac] ?? 'planned'}` })\n    }\n  }\n\n  errors.push(...checkDefects({ root, index, milestone }).errors)\n\n  if (errors.length > 0) return errors\n\n  const coveredFiles = collectCoveredFiles(root)\n  const outDir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'cliworker-gate-'))\n  try {\n    const result = spawnSync(process.execPath, [RUN_TESTS, '--root', root, '--out-dir', outDir], {\n      cwd: root,\n      encoding: 'utf8',\n      maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024,\n    })\n    if (result.status !== 0) {\n      const tail = (result.stderr || result.stdout || '').trim().split('\\n').slice(-3).join(' | ')\n      errors.push({ id: 'gate.run_failed', detail: `full run exited ${result.status}${tail ? `: ${tail}` : ''}` })\n      return errors\n    }\n\n    let manifest = null\n    try {\n=== fixtures m2 defects.json ===\n{\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"a3-runner\",\n      \"owner_task\": \"T-001\",\n      \"owner_milestone\": \"M1\",\n      \"ac\": \"AC-001\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"a5-shell\",\n      \"owner_task\": \"T-010\",\n      \"owner_milestone\": \"M2\",\n      \"ac\": \"AC-026\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}}}],"role":"user"},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b6e5ba867444bd771672006700a3","_messageId":"01a1b6e6077576838d10c6aa595dd0f7","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b6e5ba867444bd771672006700a3","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b6e6077576838d10c6aa595dd0f7","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b6e6077576838d10c6aa595dd0f7","traceparent":"00-4a75c094774db18b4fa9b0e599d0db42-f8dbe608f8e504b0-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b6e5-b62d-77fa-b8d6-e09057b99f7b,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b6e5ba867444bd771672006700a3"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T16:44:54.024Z"}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b6e6-138e-70eb-b2d4-7e705e8a6758","session_id":"01a1b6e5-b62d-77fa-b8d6-e09057b99f7b","message":{"id":"01a1b6e6138e70ebb2d47e6eec58f311","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Important: T-001 test at line 54-62 calls `runDefects(['--root', M2_PLANNED])` and expects \"check-defects: ok\". With my change: --root without --strict → not strict → OK, unchanged. Good.\n\nTest at line 64: `--milestone M1 --root` → not strict → set_mismatch only when milestone==='M5'. Good.\n\nNow, important design point §5.6: \"严格模式在原有检查之外还做：1. 账本 id 集合必须恰好是 11 个预期 id（复用 defects.set_mismatch，不论是否带 --milestone）。2. 对每一条缺陷调用 checkRepros。\"\n\nWait — in strict mode on the real repo: checkDefects({root, index, milestone: null}) computes `required` = valid defects whose AC is covered in merged shards. Our defects' ACs: AC-015, AC-025, AC-001, AC-012, AC-014, AC-020, AC-020, AC-022, AC-024, AC-026, AC-055. Which are covered? Only AC-001 (T-001's shard) and AC-005 (mine). So required = [a3-runner] only, which has a [fixed] section. Good — so no `defects.missing_fixed` for the other 10.\n\nLet me confirm: mergeShards requires each covered AC's files to exist and have plain title containing [AC]. T-001.json covers AC-001..004, AC-074. Our T-002.json covers AC-005 with files stable.test.mjs and test/unit/frozen-files.test.mjs. Both must exist and have a plain title containing `[AC-005]`.\n\nCareful: `check-traceability` will check shard T-002.json: task must be \"T-002\", owner of AC-005 is T-002. Good. Files exist. Titles present.\n\nWait — a problem: T-002.json `files` includes `test/characterization/stable.test.mjs` and `test/unit/frozen-files.test.mjs`. Both must exist because mergeShards checks fs.existsSync. I'll create both.\n\nNow E-1 requires `Object.keys(j).sort().join()==='entries,milestone,task'` — so only those 3 keys.\n\nNow the big piece: check-frozen-files.mjs, check-defects additions, 10 repro files, 11 canaries, stable.test.mjs, 15 unit tests.\n\nLet me carefully design.\n\n### Hash definition\nsha256 of content with `\\r\\n` → `\\n`. Implement:\n```js\nfunction normalizedHash(buffer) {\n  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer.toString('latin1').replace(/\\r\\n/g, '\\n'), 'latin1').digest('hex')\n}\n```\nUsing latin1 preserves bytes exactly. Note: buffer.toString('latin1') then replace then update with 'latin1' — byte-exact. Good. This matches B-1's approach.\n\n### frozen-files.json content\n\nFiles registered:\n- test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/lib/availability.mjs\n- .../lib/cli.mjs\n- .../lib/config.mjs\n- .../lib/forecast.mjs\n- .../lib/index.js\n- .../lib/select.mjs\n- .../config/cliworker.example.json\n- test/characterization/stable.test.mjs\n- contracts/spec/** existing files (currently only ac-index.json — excluded)\n- contracts/golden/** (none)\n\nSo: 8 entries. A-2 requires N ≥ 8. Good.\n\nDoes check-frozen-files validate that `contracts/spec/**` files that exist are registered? No — spec says only registered must be unchanged, plus copy dir completeness + stable must be registered.\n\n### check-frozen-files.mjs\n\n```js\nexport function checkFrozenFiles({ root }) -> { errors: [{id, detail}] }\n```\nCareful: unit test #1 expects `errors` array. Also acceptance output format.\n\nAllowed set:\n- prefix `test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/`\n- exact `test/characterization/stable.test.mjs`\n- prefix `contracts/spec/`\n- prefix `contracts/golden/`\n\nOrder of judgment per design §4: normalize, then forbidden_path, then outside_allowed, then read file.\n\nNormalization details:\n- Absolute path → outside_allowed (need check before normalization since path.posix.normalize of \"C:/x\" stays). Design: \"是绝对路径、含 `..`、含反斜杠或盘符\" → outside_allowed.\n- Backslash → outside_allowed. But careful: check forbidden_path for `lib\\select.mjs`? Design says backslash is illegal → outside_allowed. Design's order: normalize (`\\` → 视为非法, `path.posix.normalize`), 再判 forbidden_path, 再判 outside_allowed. Hmm ambiguous: backslash treated as illegal... \"先规范化（\\→ 视为非法，path.posix.normalize）\". I read it as: backslash is illegal (reject), and also do posix normalize. Which error id for backslash? §4 table: `frozen.outside_allowed` includes \"含反斜杠或盘符\". So backslash → outside_allowed. But if the path starts with `lib\\`, is it forbidden_path? To keep it simple and match table: determine forbidden_path first (case-insensitive prefix check on the raw path with `/` separators — but backslash path `lib\\select.mjs` starts with `lib\\` not `lib/`). Then outside_allowed for backslash.\n\nHmm, but B-4 only tests `lib/select.mjs` etc with forward slashes. Unit test #9 tests traversal and absolute → expect outside_allowed. Unit test #4 tests `lib/`, `bin/`, `config/` → forbidden_path.\n\nSo implement:\n1. If typeof path !== 'string' or empty → manifest_schema? Design says entry missing path → manifest_schema. Handled earlier in schema validation.\n2. `raw = path`\n3. Determine `badChars`: contains '\\\\' → outside_allowed. Or /^[A-Za-z]:/ drive letter → outside_allowed. Or path.posix.isAbsolute(raw) → outside_allowed.\n4. normalized = posix.normalize(raw); if normalized starts with '..' or has '..' segment → outside_allowed.\n5. If /^(lib|bin|config)\\//i.test(normalized) → forbidden_path (detail = normalized? B-4 expects `FAIL frozen.forbidden_path lib/select.mjs` — detail is the path as given/normalized).\n\nCareful: order matters for `lib/../x`? Not tested. I'll do: backslash/drive/absolute check first (outside_allowed), then normalize, then `..` check (outside_allowed), then forbidden prefix, then allowed-set.\n\nHmm but design says: forbidden before outside_allowed. For `lib/select.mjs` both are... no: `lib/select.mjs` is not in allowed set, so it'd be outside_allowed too. Must emit forbidden_path. So forbidden check comes before the allowed-set check. That's the essential ordering. My order: format checks → forbidden → allowed set. Fine. But design's B-4 expects scripts/run-tests.mjs → outside_allowed. Yes.\n\nWait, careful: would `lib/` prefix check catch `test/fixtures/.../lib/select.mjs`? No — starts with `test/`.\n\nAlso `frozen.outside_allowed` for paths containing `..`: `path.posix.normalize('test/../lib/x')` = 'lib/x' → then forbidden_path? Design lists `..` under outside_allowed. So check `..` before forbidden. Unit test #9 expects outside_allowed for traversal. I'll check literal `..` segments before normalize.\n\n6. For manifest validation:\n- file missing / invalid JSON → `frozen.manifest_missing` and return.\n- not object, or `files` not array → manifest_schema.\n- `source_commit !== 'f75bb76'` → manifest_schema.\n- each entry: object, `path` string non-empty, `sha256` matches /^[0-9a-f]{64}$/, duplicate path → manifest_schema.\n- Do we validate entry keys exactly `path,sha256`? B-2 does on the real file. Design says \"条目缺 path/sha256 ... 或路径重复\". Not extra keys. I won't reject extra keys (avoid over-engineering), but I also won't add any.\n\n7. For each valid-path entry: resolve to root + path. If !exists → `frozen.file_missing`. Else hash compare → `frozen.hash_mismatch`.\n\nCareful with B-3: after tampering select.mjs, error must be exactly `FAIL frozen.hash_mismatch test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/lib/select.mjs` and total 1 error. Tampering with Add-Content — adds bytes. Good.\n\nBut careful: B-3 copies `test` and `contracts`. Then hash of stable.test.mjs: unchanged. OK. Number of registered files 8, none missing. Only 1 error.\n\n8. `frozen.unregistered`: walk `test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/` under root; any file not in registered set → error with detail = posix relative path from root. Also if `test/characterization/stable.test.mjs` not registered → unregistered with that path.\n\nAlso: if the copy dir doesn't exist at all? Design doesn't say. Then no unregistered errors; file_missing will fire for each registered. Fine.\n\nB-5: creates `test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/README.md` → `FAIL frozen.unregistered test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/README.md`. Total errors: 1 (only that), and output `check-frozen-files: FAILED (1 errors)`. B-5 only checks the unregistered line. Fine.\n\nOutput:\n- ok: `check-frozen-files: ok (N files)` where N = registered file count.\n- fail: lines + `check-frozen-files: FAILED (n errors)`.\n- unknown args → exit 2.\n\nCLI args: `--root <dir>` only. Support `--root` repeated? No.\n\nNow, `--root $t` in B-3 where $t is a temp dir with test/ and contracts/. Our script resolves root. Note check-frozen-files is at scripts/check-frozen-files.mjs in the *repo*, and `--root $t`. Good.\n\nNow unit test file needs `checkFrozenFiles({root})` with mini roots.\n\n### stable.test.mjs\n\nImports: `../fixtures/characterization/v0.1/lib/select.mjs` (relative from test/characterization/). That contains `fixtures/characterization/v0.1/`. Also `../fixtures/.../lib/config.mjs`. But note config.mjs SCHEMA. And `forecast.mjs` maybe not needed.\n\nC-1 regex: `/(?:from\\s*|import\\s*\\(\\s*)[\\x27\\x22](\\.[^\\x27\\x22]*)[\\x27\\x22]/g` — collects relative specifiers, requires length>=2 and all include `fixtures/characterization/v0.1/`. And must NOT contain `'../../lib/` etc.\n\nTest 6 `[AC-005] stable: imports only the v0.1 copy`: read own source, all specs starting with '.' must include `fixtures/characterization/v0.1/`.\n\nSo in stable.test.mjs I must import only from the copy. Node built-ins like `node:test`, `node:fs`, `node:path`, `node:url` are fine (not starting with '.').\n\nNow the six test cases. Let me design fixtures and verify against 0.1 behavior.\n\nConfig fixture:\n```js\nconst config = {\n  providers: { claude: { cli: 'claude', readonly: true } },\n  models: { ... },\n}\nconst status = { claude: { state: 'callable' } }\n```\n\nTest 1: deprecated models never candidates.\nmodels:\n- 'claude-sonnet' { provider:'claude', id:'sonnet', level:'Quick', identity:'verified' }\n- 'claude-old' { provider:'claude', id:'old', level:'Quick', identity:'verified', deprecated:true, deprecated_reason:'retired' }\n\nlistModels(config, {status}) → only claude-sonnet. With includeDeprecated:true → both, and old has availability 'deprecated'.\n\nselectModels(config, {level:'Quick'}, {status}) → rejected contains {key:'claude-old', reason:'availability:deprecated'}, models keys don't include claude-old.\n\nCheck code: listModels filters !deprecated unless includeDeprecated. selectModels uses includeDeprecated:true. candidates filter order: exclude, allowed, level, callable, requireVerified... For claude-old: level Quick === level → passes; callable = st.state==='callable' && !m.deprecated → false; availability = m.deprecated ? 'deprecated' : st.state → 'deprecated'. rejected reason 'availability:deprecated'. \n\nTest 2: unverified identity rejected unless require_verified false.\nmodel 'claude-unv' { provider:'claude', id:'unv', level:'Quick', identity:'unverified' }. Note listModels maps identity: m.identity === 'verified' ? 'verified':'unverified'. selectModels with {level:'Quick'} → rejected reason 'identity_unverified'. With require_verified:false → picked includes it.\n\nCareful: with two models in same config, count defaults 1. Use separate config or count:2. I'll build per-test configs to keep it clear.\n\nTest 3: level filter rejects other levels. request level 'top' (lowercase). Quick model rejected reason `level:Quick`. Missing level → ok:false, reason 'E_LEVEL_REQUIRED'.\n\nTest 4: exclude wins. request.exclude:[key] → reason 'excluded' even though level would reject. So use a Top-level model and request level:'Quick', exclude that model's key → reason excluded. And another Quick model gets picked.\n\nWait: \"一个本应 `level:` 被拒的 key 被 exclude 时原因仍是 `excluded`\". Yes.\n\nTest 5: four schema names.\n- config.mjs SCHEMA === 'cliworker.config/v1'\n- select.mjs SCHEMA === 'cliworker.selection/v1'\n- listModels entry schema 'cliworker.model/v1'\n- handle schema 'cliworker.handle/v1'\n\nTest 6: imports.\n\nAlso note selectModels requires `config.providers` for handles: `adapter: config.providers[m.provider]?.adapter || null`. Fine.\n\nNow — careful with test 4 & forecast: no usage → forecast with records [] → status unknown, risk undefined! Look: forecast returns `{status:'unknown', samples:0, reason:'no usage samples', burn_per_task:null, tasks_left:null, eta_hours:null, confidence:'none'}` — no `risk` key. So f.risk === undefined. In candidates filter, usageKind normal → skip. rank: unknown = f.risk==='unknown' ? 1 : 0 → 0. Fine.\n\n`quota` undefined → forecast handles.\n\nNow `cliCall` uses `request.cwd || '<workspace>'`. For claude provider returns command: p.cli. Fine.\n\n### repro files\n\nEach repro file structure per §5.3:\n```js\nconst V01 = process.env.CLIWORKER_V01_ROOT\n  ?? fileURLToPath(new URL('../../fixtures/characterization/v0.1/', import.meta.url))\n```\nFile is at test/characterization/defects/x.test.mjs, so `../../fixtures/...` = test/fixtures/... Correct.\n\nD-3 checks each file contains 'CLIWORKER_V01_ROOT'.\n\n[repro] block format:\n- line starts at column 0 with `test('[repro]` and ends with `{`.\n- ends at first line that is exactly `})`.\n\nNote: `test('[repro] [AC-005] ...', async () => {` ends with `{`. Good. Must be at line start with no indentation.\n\nD-3: `opens.some(l=>!/\\{\\s*$/.test(l))` → must end with `{`.\n\nAnd `/^\\}\\)$/m` → must have a line exactly `})`. Multiple blocks each ending `})` — fine.\n\nAlso must NOT contain `[fixed]` anywhere in the file (D-3 `if(/\\[fixed\\]/.test(s))bad.push('fixed '+id)`). Careful: don't mention \"[fixed]\" in comments either.\n\nAlso note: repro title must be `[repro] [AC-005] ...` so run-tests --ac AC-005 picks the file, and A-1 expects `[repro] [AC-005]` titles to appear (10 of them). Actually A-1 says \"stdout 含 10 个以 `[repro] [AC-005]` 开头的标题\" — need to count? It just says contains titles starting with that. I'll ensure all 10 have that prefix.\n\nInner blocks: self-contained — needed constants inside the block.\n\nIndentation inside block is fine (only the opening line must be unindented).\n\nImportant: `extractReproSection` uses opening line detection: line starts with `test('[repro]` or `test(\"[repro]` AND ends with `{`. So block bodies must not contain a line at column 0 starting with `test('[repro]...{`. Fine.\n\nNow let's write each repro and think about canary correctness.\n\n#### a1-quota\nRepro: forecast from copy `lib/forecast.mjs`.\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] quota with remaining_pct yields unknown risk and no remaining_fraction', async () => {\n  const { forecast } = await import(pathToFileURL(path.join(V01, 'lib', 'forecast.mjs')).href)\n  const now = Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1, 12)\n  const records = [0,1,2].map((i) => ({ model:'m', at: new Date(now - (i+1) * 10 * 60_000).toISOString(), cost_usd: 0.5 }))\n  const a = forecast({ records, model:'m', quota: { remaining_pct: 5 }, now })\n  assert.equal(a.risk, 'unknown')\n  assert.equal(a.tasks_left, null)\n  assert.equal('remaining_fraction' in a, false)\n  assert.equal('risk_basis' in a, false)\n  const b = forecast({ records, model:'m', quota: { remaining: 1, unit: 'credits' }, now })\n  assert.equal(b.risk, 'unknown')\n  assert.equal('reason' in b, false)\n})\n```\nWait: b — records non-empty so samples exist, returns status 'known', no `reason` key. Right, the `reason` key only in the no-sample branch. Since samples exist, `'reason' in b` is false. Good.\n\nHmm but careful: with `{remaining:1, unit:'credits'}`: quota.remaining_usd undefined → not finite... `Number.isFinite(Number(undefined))` → Number(undefined)=NaN → false. Then `Number.isFinite(Number(1)) && 1>0 && tokens>0 && quota.unit==='tokens'` → unit is 'credits' → false. tasksLeft null → risk 'unknown'. Good.\n\nAlso `assert.equal(a.risk,'unknown')`: tasksLeft null → risk 'unknown'. Good.\n\nCanary overlay lib/forecast.mjs: recognize remaining_pct, output remaining_fraction and risk 'low_reserve'. Then `a.risk==='unknown'` fails. Good.\n\n#### a2-pack\n```js\nconst V01_PACKAGE = { bin: null, files: ['lib','cordis.patch.yml','dsh.plugin.json','README.md'] }\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] 0.1 package has no bin and ships without bin or config', async () => {\n  assert.equal(V01_PACKAGE.bin, null)\n  assert.ok(!V01_PACKAGE.files.includes('bin'))\n  assert.ok(!V01_PACKAGE.files.includes('config'))\n  assert.ok(fs.existsSync(path.join(V01, 'config', 'cliworker.example.json')))\n})\n```\ncanary repro_edits: find `bin: null` replace `bin: 'bin/cliworker.mjs'`. Must appear exactly once in the repro file source. Careful: `bin: null` also appears... in the test title? No. In `files.includes('bin')`? no. OK. Note: `assert.equal(V01_PACKAGE.bin, null)` contains `bin, null` not `bin: null`. Good.\n\nHmm, but repro_edits applied to the copied repro file. After edit: `bin: 'bin/cliworker.mjs'` → assert.equal(...bin, null) fails → not ok. Good.\n\nBut careful: `find` must appear exactly once — check in `checkRepros`. Note the edited file still has the [repro] title so `--test-name-pattern` matches.\n\n#### a3-runner\nT-001's file; canary `repro_edits` find `scripts: null,` replace `scripts: { test: 'node scripts/run-tests.mjs' },`. Must appear exactly once in a3-runner.test.mjs. Line 16: `  scripts: null,`. Yes appears once. But also line 28 `assert.ok(pkg.scripts, ...)` — no.\n\nWait — the a3 repro asserts `assert.equal(V01.scripts, null)`. With edit, scripts is object → fail. Good.\n\nAlso baseline: a3 repro passes on copy. Yes (`V01.testFiles` inline).\n\nHmm: a3-runner repro title is `[repro] 0.1 has no test script and a test file outside the discovery roots` — no [AC-005]. Good; checkRepros uses --test-name-pattern=\\[repro\\] which matches.\n\n#### a4-paths\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] config home does not follow the config file directory', async () => {\n  const os = require? no — ESM\n```\nNeed dynamic import of node:fs etc. Since we can't static import inside block? We can — but block must be self-contained; static imports at top are allowed as long as they're outside the block (block sha only covers block). But \"块内 try/finally 还原环境变量并清理\" and \"块外定义 V01\". Constants inside block allowed.\n\nI'd rather put all node imports at top of file (file-level), which is fine; blocks use them. But the design says each block self-contained (需要的常量、夹具、清理都写在块里). I'll put imports at top (needed for V01 anyway) and put fixtures/temp logic inside blocks.\n\na4-paths repro:\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] config _home ignores the config file directory', async () => {\n  const { loadConfig } = await import(pathToFileURL(path.join(V01, 'lib', 'config.mjs')).href)\n  const base = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'cw-a4-paths-'))\n  const dirA = path.join(base, 'a')\n  const dirB = path.join(base, 'b')\n  const home = path.join(base, 'home')\n  fs.mkdirSync(dirA, { recursive: true })\n  fs.mkdirSync(dirB, { recursive: true })\n  fs.mkdirSync(home, { recursive: true })\n  const fileA = path.join(dirA, 'config.json')\n  const fileB = path.join(dirB, 'config.json')\n  fs.writeFileSync(fileA, JSON.stringify({ providers: {}, models: {} }))\n  fs.writeFileSync(fileB, JSON.stringify({ providers: {}, models: {} }))\n  const previous = process.env.CLIWORKER_HOME\n  process.env.CLIWORKER_HOME = home\n  try {\n    const a = loadConfig({ file: fileA })\n    const b = loadConfig({ file: fileB })\n    assert.equal(a._home, b._home)\n    assert.equal(path.resolve(a._home).startsWith(path.resolve(dirA) + path.sep), false)\n    assert.equal(path.resolve(b._home).startsWith(path.resolve(dirB) + path.sep), false)\n  } finally {\n    if (previous === undefined) delete process.env.CLIWORKER_HOME\n    else process.env.CLIWORKER_HOME = previous\n    fs.rmSync(base, { recursive: true, force: true })\n  }\n})\n```\nNote: `defaultHome()` reads env at call time. Good.\n\nWindows case-insensitivity: `startsWith` on resolved paths — fine since same base.\n\nCanary overlay lib/config.mjs: `_home` = path.join(dirname(file), 'state'). Then a._home is under dirA → assertion fails → not ok. Good.\n\n#### a4-null-config\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] invalid config errors carry no code', async () => {\n  const { loadConfig } = await import(...)\n  const base = fs.mkdtempSync(...)\n  const f1 = path.join(base,'null.json'); fs.writeFileSync(f1, 'null')\n  const f2 = path.join(base,'empty.json'); fs.writeFileSync(f2, '{}')\n  try {\n    let err = null\n    try { loadConfig({ file: f1 }) } catch (e) { err = e }\n    assert.ok(err instanceof TypeError)\n    assert.equal(err.code, undefined)\n    let err2 = null\n    try { loadConfig({ file: f2 }) } catch (e) { err2 = e }\n    assert.ok(err2 instanceof Error)\n    assert.equal(err2.code, undefined)\n  } finally { fs.rmSync(base,{recursive:true,force:true}) }\n})\n```\nCheck 0.1: f1='null' → JSON.parse → raw=null → `!raw` true → throw new Error(`unsupported CLIWorker config schema: ${raw.schema}`) → but `raw.schema` where raw is null → TypeError \"Cannot read properties of null\". Yes TypeError, code undefined. \n\nf2='{}' → raw={} truthy, no schema → `!raw.providers` → throw new Error('providers is required'), code undefined. Good.\n\nCanary overlay config.mjs: all throws get code='E_CONFIG' and avoid null property access → assertions fail. Make sure both throw with code. In canary: if (!raw) throw error with code E_CONFIG; if (!raw.providers) throw with code E_CONFIG. Both → err.code defined → first assertion `err.code === undefined` fails. Note first check `err instanceof TypeError` would fail if we throw Error instead. Either way fails → not ok. Good. But must be a *failure* with `not ok ... [repro]` — assertion error, not a load error. Yes.\n\n#### a5-dup-call\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] the handle duplicates the call object as cli', async () => {\n  const { selectModels } = await import(...)\n  const config = { providers: { claude: { cli: 'claude', readonly: true } }, models: { 'claude-quick': { provider:'claude', id:'q', level:'Quick', identity:'verified' } } }\n  const status = { claude: { state: 'callable' } }\n  const result = selectModels(config, { level: 'Quick' }, { status })\n  assert.equal(result.ok, true)\n  const h = result.models[0]\n  assert.deepEqual(Object.keys(h).filter(k=>k==='cli'||k==='call').sort(), ['call','cli'])\n  assert.deepEqual(h.cli, h.call)\n})\n```\nSimpler: `assert.ok('cli' in h)` and `assert.ok('call' in h)` and deepEqual. Canary: overlay select.mjs without `cli: call`. Then 'cli' in h false → fail.\n\nCareful: for overlay select.mjs I need the full file minus `cli: call,`. And the overlay must not break other things.\n\n#### a5-forecast-x3\nRepro: config.quotas with Proxy counting gets on provider key.\n\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] forecast reads provider quota once per model per pass', async () => {\n  const { selectModels } = await import(...)\n  let reads = 0\n  const quotas = new Proxy({}, {\n    get(target, prop) {\n      if (prop === 'claude') reads += 1\n      return target[prop]\n    },\n  })\n  const models = {}\n  for (let i = 0; i < 12; i += 1) models[`claude-m${i}`] = { provider:'claude', id:`m${i}`, level:'Quick', identity:'verified', prices:{input_per_mtok:1, output_per_mtok:1} }\n  const config = { providers: { claude: { cli:'claude', readonly:true } }, models, quotas }\n  const result = selectModels(config, { level:'Quick', count: 12 }, { status: { claude: { state:'callable' } } })\n  assert.equal(result.models.length, 12)\n  assert.ok(reads > 12, `reads=${reads}`)\n})\n```\nHmm — careful: `config.quotas?.[m.provider]` → get trap for 'claude'. listModels: no quota access. In candidates filter: forecast per model (1 read each = 12). rank comparator: each call to rank does 1 read; number of comparator invocations for 12 items varies (Array.sort). Actually rank is called inside comparator, so comparisons × 2 reads. Then picked map: 1 read each for 12 = 12. Total > 12 definitely.\n\nBut careful: `config.quotas` must be the Proxy, and `config.quotas?.[m.provider]` works. Also handle `quota: config.quotas?.[m.provider] || null`.\n\nCanary overlay select.mjs: memoize per model key and reuse for the handle's quota such that reads === 12 exactly. So compute forecast once per model key, cache in Map; handle quota reuses cached quota read. Then assert reads > 12 fails.\n\nImplementation for canary overlay: modify select.mjs so that quota is read once per model key in a memo cache:\n\n```js\nconst quotaCache = new Map()\nconst quotaOf = (provider) => { if (!quotaCache.has(provider)) quotaCache.set(provider, config.quotas?.[provider] || null); return quotaCache.get(provider) }\n```\nHmm but \"按模型 key 记忆化，且 handle 的 quota 也复用同一次读取，使次数恰好等于 12\". If memoized by provider, reads = 1, not 12. Need exactly 12 → memoize per model key: each model key reads once total. So:\n\n```js\nconst memo = new Map()\nfunction quotaFor(m) {\n  if (!memo.has(m.key)) memo.set(m.key, config.quotas?.[m.provider] || null)\n  return memo.get(m.key)\n}\n```\nThen 12 models × 1 read = 12. And `reads > 12` false. \n\nBut careful: the Proxy get trap counts only 'claude' property gets. If memoized per key, exactly 12. Good.\n\nAlso handle `quota: quotaFor(m)` and forecast quota: quotaFor(m).\n\n#### a5-unused\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] select.mjs keeps a dead isObject helper and cli.mjs an unused fs import', async () => {\n  const select = fs.readFileSync(path.join(V01, 'lib', 'select.mjs'), 'utf8')\n  assert.equal(select.split('isObject').length - 1, 1)\n  const cli = fs.readFileSync(path.join(V01, 'lib', 'cli.mjs'), 'utf8')\n  assert.ok(/import fs from/.test(cli))\n  assert.equal(cli.includes('fs.'), false)\n})\n```\nCheck 0.1 select.mjs: `isObject` appears once at line 7 (`function isObject(v)`). Search: line 7 only. Yes shown in file: only line 7. Good.\n\ncli.mjs: `import fs from 'node:fs'` yes; `fs.` — check content: line 1 import, no `fs.` usage anywhere. Good.\n\nCanary overlay: select.mjs without isObject, cli.mjs without fs import. Both overlays. Then `isObject` count 0 → fail.\n\nWait: overlay select.mjs must still work when imported? The repro for a5-unused only reads source, doesn't import. But checkRepros for a5-unused runs only that repro file. Fine.\n\nHmm, but careful: overlay cli.mjs — must valid syntax. Fine.\n\n#### a5-ignored-fields\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] unknown request fields are silently ignored', async () => {\n  const { selectModels } = await import(...)\n  const config = { providers: { claude: { cli:'claude', readonly:true } }, models: { 'claude-quick': { provider:'claude', id:'q', level:'Quick', identity:'verified' } } }\n  const result = selectModels(config, { level:'Quick', role:'dev', importance:'high' }, { status: { claude: { state:'callable' } } })\n  assert.equal(result.ok, true)\n  assert.equal('warnings' in result, false)\n})\n```\nCanary overlay select.mjs returns warnings array → `'warnings' in result` true → fail.\n\n#### a5-shell\n```js\ntest('[repro] [AC-005] availability runs commands through a shell on win32', async () => {\n  const source = fs.readFileSync(path.join(V01, 'lib', 'availability.mjs'), 'utf8')\n  assert.match(source, /shell:\\s*process\\.platform\\s*===\\s*'win32'/)\n})\n```\nCanary overlay availability.mjs with `shell: false` → regex fails.\n\nCareful: overlay availability.mjs must keep other behavior (a5-nocache canary? no, separate). Fine.\n\n#### a5-nocache\nRepro: write probe.mjs in temp dir, chdir, provider {cli:'node', version_args:['probe.mjs']}, call providerStatus(config) twice, file length 2.\n\nprobe script:\n```js\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nfs.appendFileSync(process.env.CLIWORKER_TEST_PROBE_FILE, 'x')\nprocess.stdout.write('node 1.0.0\\n')\n```\nWait — spawn with shell:true on win32: command 'node', args ['probe.mjs']. With shell true, command line is built as `node probe.mjs`. cwd of spawnSync = process.cwd() (inherited). We chdir. Good.\n\nBut `shell: process.platform === 'win32'` → on win32 shell true; on linux shell false. Either way node probe.mjs works (PATH resolution fine).\n\nversion_args: ['probe.mjs'] → run(cli='node', ['probe.mjs']). stdout 'node 1.0.0' → version match /\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+/ → '1.0.0'. Then no auth_args → `{ state: 'callable', ... }`.\n\nproviderStatus(config) loops config.providers entries. Test file is ESM in temp dir? The probe.mjs is ESM with `import fs` — needs package.json type module in temp dir OR use .cjs. Node 20: .mjs is always ESM. Fine, use probe.mjs with import.\n\nLength of probe file after 2 calls = 2. Canary overlay availability.mjs: cache status per provider config → length 1.\n\nCaching key: JSON.stringify(provider) maybe. Careful: cache must key on provider config so both calls hit cache. But also must not break the first call. Use a WeakMap keyed on provider object? providerStatus passes the same object `p` both times (config.providers.claude). WeakMap on object identity works. Simpler & robust.\n\nNote: repro must assert length === 2. And cleanup: restore cwd and env, rm temp dir.\n\nCareful: `process.chdir` in a test process — node --test runs each file in a separate process by default, so safe. Also must restore.\n\nPotential issue: the probe file path passed via env var — spawn inherits env (spawnSync without env option inherits process.env). Yes.\n\nAlso `CLIWORKER_TEST_PROBE_FILE` must be set before calls. Use unique temp dir.\n\nCanary for a5-nocache: overlay availability.mjs with WeakMap cache → length 1 → assertion fails.\n\n#### a4-... etc.\n\nNow checkRepros implementation details.\n\nExtract repro section:\n\n```js\nexport function extractReproSection(source) {\n  const text = String(source ?? '').replace(/\\r\\n/g, '\\n')\n  const lines = text.split('\\n')\n  const blocks = []\n  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n    const line = lines[i]\n    if (!/^test\\(['\"`]\\[repro\\]/.test(line)) continue\n    if (!/\\{\\s*$/.test(line)) continue   // not a block start\n    let end = -1\n    for (let j = i + 1; j < lines.length; j += 1) {\n      if (lines[j] === '})') { end = j; break }\n    }\n    if (end < 0) return { blocks, error: ... }\n    blocks.push(lines.slice(i, end + 1).join('\\n'))\n    i = end\n  }\n  return blocks\n}\n```\n\nHmm, design says signature `extractReproSection(source)`; returns what? Unit tests / checkRepros need both blocks and format errors. Design: \"导出 extractReproSection(source) 和 reproSha256(source)\". Simplest: extractReproSection returns array of block strings; malformed detection separate. But `defects.repro_format` needs \"某块的起始行不以 { 结尾 / 没有结束的 })\". \n\nLet me return an object? Acceptance doesn't test the shape directly (no acceptance command imports it except maybe unit tests I write). Design doesn't specify return type. I'll make `extractReproSection(source)` return `{ blocks: string[], problems: string[] }`? Hmm — risky if a grader checks. There's no acceptance check on it. But the design says \"实现者用它们生成登记值；同一个函数被检查使用\". I'll keep it simple: return array of blocks, and throw? No.\n\nI'll return `{ blocks, malformed }`... Let me think about which is most natural for \"extract the section text\". Hmm. I'll go with returning an object `{ blocks, errors }` where errors are strings — documented in impl.md. Since no acceptance constraint, fine.\n\nHmm, actually simpler and safer for \"段文本\" semantics: return the joined text? But then can't detect format problems. I'll do `{ blocks, errors }`.\n\n`reproSha256(source)`:\n```js\nexport function reproSha256(source) {\n  const { blocks, errors } = extractReproSection(source)\n  if (errors.length) return null\n  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(blocks.join('\\n'), 'utf8').digest('hex')\n}\n```\nEach block already CRLF-normalized. Joined with '\\n'.\n\nWait: \"段文本 = 文件中全部 [repro] 块（按出现顺序），每块先把 CRLF 换成 LF，块之间用 \\n 连接\". Yes.\n\nNow checkRepros:\n\n```js\nexport function checkRepros({ root, defects, nodePath = process.execPath })\n```\n`defects` = array of ledger entries (with id, repro_sha256).\n\nFor each defect:\n1. sha present & 64 hex else `defects.repro_sha_missing` detail = id.\n2. read repro file `test/characterization/defects/<id>.test.mjs`; missing → `defects.repro_missing`.\n3. extractReproSection: errors → `defects.repro_format`; blocks.length===0 → `defects.repro_missing`.\n4. sha compare → `defects.repro_changed`.\n5. canary: read `test/characterization/canary/<id>/canary.json`; missing or invalid or `defect !== id` → `defects.canary_missing`.\n6. overlay dir: collect files under canary/<id>/overlay recursively; each relative path must be within V01? \"overlay 路径越界\" → `defects.canary_error`. Overlay paths are relative to copy root; validate no `..`, not absolute, no backslash, and must be in allowed set? \"overlay 路径越界\" means escaping. I'll check: normalized path has no `..`, not absolute, no backslash, and startsWith? no prefix constraint besides within copy. Just no traversal.\n7. `repro_edits`: array (optional); each item `{find, replace}` strings; `find` count in repro source === 1 else `defects.canary_error`.\n8. at least one overlay file or one repro_edit → else `defects.canary_missing`.\n9. baseline: run → must pass.\n10. canary: run → must fail.\n\nSkip running baseline/canary if an earlier structural error occurred for that defect (to avoid noise and time). E.g. if repro_changed, still could run? Design lists steps sequentially; I think it's fine to skip runs when structural errors exist — but D-4 expects `FAIL defects.repro_changed a5-dup-call` and exit 1; whether canary also runs doesn't matter. But careful: in D-4 the [repro] title gets edited to `[repro] edited`, so name pattern `\\[repro\\]` still matches. Baseline would still pass. So no noise either way. But to save time, skip runs when structural error for that defect. Hmm — but D-4 output must contain the repro_changed line; skipping is fine.\n\nActually careful: if we skip baseline/canary when structural errors, then in D-5 (canary overlay replaced with original) structure is fine → baseline passes, canary survives → error. Good.\n\nLet me implement: for each defect, `let structural = false`; on structural error push and continue (skip runs). That means at most one error per defect per phase, keeping output tidy. D-5 requires \"不含对其它 10 个缺陷的 canary_survived\" — satisfied.\n\nNow run step. Build temp root:\n```js\nconst tmp = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'cliworker-canary-'))\ntry {\n  const copyRoot = path.join(tmp, 'v0.1')\n  copyDir(path.join(root, 'test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1'), copyRoot)\n  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(copyRoot, 'package.json'), '{\"type\":\"module\"}')\n  // canary: overlay\n  for (const [rel, src] of overlays) writeFileSync(path.join(copyRoot, rel), readFileSync(src))\n  // repro\n  let reproSource = readFileSync(reproFile,'utf8')\n  for (const edit of edits) reproSource = reproSource.replace(...)  // careful: count exactly once\n  // rewrite relative specifiers to file URLs\n  const rewritten = rewriteRelativeImports(reproSource, path.dirname(reproFile))\n  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(tmp, 'repro.test.mjs'), rewritten)\n  // run\n  const env = { ...process.env, CLIWORKER_V01_ROOT: copyRoot }\n  delete env.NODE_TEST_CONTEXT\n  const res = spawnSync(nodePath, ['--test','--test-reporter=tap','--test-name-pattern=\\\\[repro\\\\]', path.join(tmp,'repro.test.mjs')], { cwd: tmp, env, encoding:'utf8', timeout: 60000 })\n```\nCareful: package.json at copyRoot provides type module for `lib/index.js`. But repro.test.mjs at tmp root has `.mjs` → ESM regardless.\n\nRewrite relative imports: need to handle `from '...'`, `import('...')`, `import '...'`. Use regex:\n```js\nconst IMPORT_RE = /((?:from\\s*|import\\s*\\(\\s*|import\\s+)(['\"])(\\.[^'\"]*)\\2)/g\n```\nSimpler: replace all string literals that start with '.' in import contexts. Let me do:\n\n```js\nsource.replace(/(\\bfrom\\s*|\\bimport\\s*\\(\\s*|\\bimport\\s+)(['\"])(\\.\\.?\\/[^'\"]*)\\2/g, (m, lead, q, spec) => `${lead}${q}${pathToFileURL(path.resolve(baseDir, spec)).href}${q}`)\n```\nAlso `import.meta.url` usage in file is fine (no specifier).\n\nOur repro files use only dynamic `import(pathToFileURL(...).href)` built from V01 — no relative specifiers except possibly the `fileURLToPath(new URL('../../fixtures/...', import.meta.url))` default for V01, which is a URL literal not an import. When running from tmp, `CLIWORKER_V01_ROOT` is set, so default unused. Good — but `import.meta.url` in the copied file resolves to tmp; irrelevant.\n\nHowever: I do use static imports at the top of repro files: `import test from 'node:test'`, `node:assert/strict`, `node:fs`, `node:path`, `node:os`, `node:url`. Those are bare/node: → no rewrite needed.\n\nAcceptance F runs repro from repo path directly — works.\n\nBaseline pass判定: exit code 0, output has a line matching `/^\\s*ok \\d+ - \\[repro\\]/m`, and no `not ok`.\n\nTap output lines: `ok 1 - [repro] ...`. In node 20 tap, indentation for subtests. Top-level: `ok 1 - [repro] [AC-005] ...`. Good.\n\nNote: `--test-name-pattern` filters; the tap output then only includes matching tests. Non-matching tests appear as `# SKIP` or filtered. Fine.\n\nCareful: baseline requires at least one `ok N - [repro]`. Good since each file has exactly one repro block that passes.\n\nCanary fail判定: output has `/^\\s*not ok \\d+ - \\[repro\\]/m`.\n\nTimeout → treat as failure: spawnSync with timeout returns `error.code === 'ETIMEDOUT'` / signal SIGTERM. For canary, timeout should NOT count as canary hit (no `not ok` line) → canary_survived. Good.\n\nHmm: design says \"超时 60 秒，超时按失败处理\" — ambiguous but per the rule \"只靠退出码非 0 不算失败成立\", timeout gives no `not ok` → canary_survived. For baseline, timeout → baseline_failed. Consistent.\n\nNow: baseline failure would be an error `defects.repro_baseline_failed`. For a2-pack and a3-runner baseline uses the copy (no overlay, no edits) → should pass.\n\nWait — for a2-pack canary, edits applied only in canary run. Baseline uses unedited repro. Good.\n\nNow `checkRepros` needs both baseline and canary runs = 22 node subprocesses + npm test itself. Fine.\n\nNow writing files into overlay: `copyDir` recursive. Use a helper `copyTree(src, dest)`.\n\nNow the CLI: strict mode.\n\n```js\nfunction main() {\n  args: --root, --index, --milestone, --strict (new flag)\n  const strict = !rootGiven || strictFlag\n  ...\n  const { errors, absent } = checkDefects({ root, index, milestone })\n  let all = [...errors]\n  if (absent && !milestone) { output 'check-defects: defects.json absent, nothing to check'; exit 0 }  // existing behavior; but strict? Design says strict adds set check + checkRepros. If ledger absent... existing prints absent message. A-3 requires stdout to not contain 'absent' — our repo has the ledger, so fine. Keep existing behavior.\n  if (strict) {\n    // set check\n    ...\n    all.push(...checkRepros({ root, defects: ledger.defects }).errors)\n  }\n}\n```\nCareful: checkDefects returns only errors, not the parsed ledger. I need the defects array for checkRepros. I'll read defects.json in main again.\n\nAlso strict set check \"不论是否带 --milestone\": compare ids to EXPECTED_IDS, push `defects.set_mismatch`.\n\nCareful about duplication when milestone==='M5': checkDefects already adds set_mismatch. In strict mode with M5 we'd double-add. Not tested; but avoid duplicates by only adding in strict when milestone !== 'M5'? Design says \"复用 defects.set_mismatch，不论是否带 --milestone\". I'll guard: if (!(milestone === 'M5')) push. Hmm, but that's a subtle deviation. Simpler: add unconditionally in strict; if milestone M5 both fire → two identical lines. Not tested anywhere. But acceptance A-3 runs no milestone → single. I'll add condition to avoid duplicate only when the same error already exists:\n\n```js\nconst already = errors.some(e => e.id === 'defects.set_mismatch')\nif (!already) { ... }\n```\nThat's clean and satisfies \"不论是否带 --milestone\".\n\nIf ledger missing in strict mode: checkRepros can't run. `absent` true → existing behavior returns early with exit 0 and 'absent' message. Hmm, that would silently pass in strict. But A-3 doesn't test absence. Design doesn't say. Keep existing behavior to avoid changing T-001 (T-001 test `runDefects(['--root', M2_PLANNED])` non-strict). For strict+absent, I'll keep the existing early return. Fine — no test.\n\nHmm wait: real repo check-fast runs strict with no args. If defects.json is absent → 'absent, nothing to check' exit 0. But our repo has it. OK.\n\nNow: strict with invalid ledger JSON → checkDefects pushes schema error, returns. Then reading ledger in main fails → skip checkRepros. Fine.\n\nNow output format for repro errors: `FAIL <id> <defect-id> <detail>` e.g. `FAIL defects.repro_changed a5-dup-call <detail>`. D-4 expects stdout contains `FAIL defects.repro_changed a5-dup-call`. So detail should be... the `id` field is `defects.repro_changed`, and design says \"失败输出沿用现有风格：`FAIL <id> <defect-id> <detail>`\". Hmm, existing style is `FAIL <id> <detail>`. The design's line has three parts. I'll emit errors as `{ id: 'defects.repro_changed', detail: 'a5-dup-call <detail>' }`? That yields `FAIL defects.repro_changed a5-dup-call ...`. D-4 expects exactly that substring. And D-5 expects `FAIL defects.canary_survived a5-dup-call`, D-6 `FAIL defects.canary_missing a4-paths`, B style unaffected.\n\nSo: detail = `${id} ${message}`. Then line = `FAIL defects.repro_changed a5-dup-call <message>`. Good, and the generic printer stays `FAIL ${id} ${detail}`.\n\nHmm, but checkRepros returns errors consumed by both CLI and unit tests. Unit tests will check `errors.some(e => e.id === 'defects.canary_survived')`. Fine.\n\nLet me make detail = `${defectId}: ${message}`? Then line = `FAIL defects.canary_survived a5-dup-call: ...` — still contains `FAIL defects.canary_survived a5-dup-call`. I'll use `${defectId} ${message}`.\n\nNow unit tests (15 cases in test/unit/frozen-files.test.mjs). These build mini roots in tmpdir. Need to be careful and reasonably quick.\n\nLet me design the mini root fixture:\n\n```js\nfunction makeMiniRoot() {\n  const root = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'cw-frozen-test-'))\n  // copy: two files with known content\n  write(root, 'test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/lib/select.mjs', 'export const x = 1\\n')\n  write(root, 'test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/config/cliworker.example.json', '{}\\n')\n  write(root, 'test/characterization/stable.test.mjs', \"test('[AC-005] stable: x', () => {})\\n\")\n  write(root, 'contracts/spec/ac-index.json', '{}\\n')\n  return root\n}\n```\nThen registered files = the 3 (2 copy + stable). Do we register contracts/spec? Allowed but not required. For test 1 (passes), register the 3.\n\nHmm: but check-frozen-files' unregistered check requires all files in copy dir registered + stable registered.\n\nTest 1: `frozen: untouched fixture root passes` → errors.length === 0.\n\nTest 2: change one registered byte → hash_mismatch.\n\nTest 3: CRLF checkout: write a registered file with CRLF while hash computed on LF → passes. So: register hash for LF content, write file with CRLF → still ok. Note: must ensure no other errors — CRLF version of same file only.\n\nTest 4: add entries `lib/select.mjs`, `bin/cliworker.mjs`, `config/cliworker.example.json` → three forbidden_path errors. But careful: adding these entries to the manifest means those files must exist... no: forbidden check happens before existence. Good. Also ensure errors contain id forbidden_path with those details.\n\nTest 5: `scripts/run-tests.mjs`, `package.json` → outside_allowed.\n\nTest 6: contracts/spec/foo.json and contracts/golden/bar.json registered and existing → passes (errors 0). Need to write those files.\n\nTest 7: unregistered file in copy → errors include unregistered with that path.\n\nTest 8: remove a registered file → file_missing.\n\nTest 9: paths `../outside.json`, `C:/abs/x.json`, `lib\\\\select.mjs` → outside_allowed (3). Hmm: for `lib\\select.mjs`, my order checks backslash first → outside_allowed. Design table says backslash/盘符 → outside_allowed. Good.\n\nTest 10: `defects: well-formed fixture ledger passes` — full mini repo with a defect: repro file with a [repro] block, defects.json with correct sha, canary with overlay, run checkRepros → no errors. This runs 2 node subprocesses with a real repro. Keep it tiny.\n\nTest 11: repro edited after registration → repro_changed. (Structural error → runs skipped, fast.)\n\nTest 12: canary that still passes → canary_survived: repro `assert.ok(true)`, canary overlay file that doesn't affect → canary run passes → survived. Requires 2 subprocess runs.\n\nTest 13: missing canary → canary_missing (no runs).\n\nTest 14: repro failing on unmodified copy → baseline_failed. Need repro that fails on copy; canary must make it \"fail\" too... Baseline fails → error. Structural ok. So 1 run (baseline) then skip canary? Design order: baseline then canary. If baseline fails, we still could run canary. I'll skip canary when baseline fails to save time (return after pushing error). Then error baseline_failed present. Test 14 asserts that.\n\nTest 15: repro that cannot load → canary_survived or canary_error. Design: canary makes repro fail due to syntax error; output has no `not ok ... [repro]`. How to make canary produce a syntax error? overlay doesn't change the repro file. repro_edits could produce a syntax error: e.g. find `assert.ok(true)` replace `assert.ok(`... hmm. Better: repro_edits find a token and replace with invalid syntax, e.g. find `const value = 1` → `const value = `. Then the repro file has a syntax error → node --test fails to load → tap output has no `not ok ... [repro]`. Baseline (unedited) passes.\n\nCareful: baseline requires `ok N - [repro]` — yes. Canary: syntax error → output like `# SyntaxError` and not ok lines without matching pattern. So canary_survived. \n\nTest 15 asserts error id is `defects.canary_survived` or `defects.canary_error`.\n\nNow, those unit tests need a mini repro that's valid