der 配置缓存状态，使长度为 1\". Cache in checkProvider keyed on provider object:\n\n```js\nexport function checkProvider(provider, { probe = true } = {}) {\n  const cli = provider.cli\n  if (!probe) return { state: 'unverified', version: null, detail: 'probe disabled' }\n  const cached = STATUS_CACHE.get(provider)\n  if (cached) return cached\n  ...\n  const result = { state: ..., ... }\n  STATUS_CACHE.set(provider, result)\n  return result\n}\n```\nSimplest: wrap the existing body? Since I'm generating via replacement, I'll rewrite the whole checkProvider function by replacing the return statements... Easier: regenerate availability.mjs overlay by full rewrite from scratch (it's short, ~38 lines). I'll write it out as a complete file. Same for config.mjs overlays (short).\n\nActually, for reliability, I'd rather hand-write the short overlays (config.mjs, availability.mjs, forecast.mjs) fully, and use node -e replacements for the long select.mjs copies.\n\nHmm, but forecast.mjs overlay also is moderate (50 lines). I'll hand-write it fully.\n\nLet me now write all 10 repro files, then generate overlays.\n\nLet me write them.\n\nFor a5-nocache probe, note: node's `--test` runs the file; inside, we chdir. The probe script path: I write `probe.mjs` into the temp dir and pass `version_args: ['probe.mjs']`, cwd = temp dir. Since `run` uses spawnSync without cwd option → inherits process.cwd() = temp dir. Good.\n\nBut wait: on win32, shell:true. spawnSync('node', ['probe.mjs'], {shell:true}) builds `node probe.mjs`. Fine.\n\nNow the probe writes to process.env.CLIWORKER_TEST_PROBE_FILE. spawnSync inherits env by default (env: process.env effectively). Yes, spawnSync without env inherits.\n\nProbe content:\n```js\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nfs.appendFileSync(process.env.CLIWORKER_TEST_PROBE_FILE, 'x')\nprocess.stdout.write('node 1.0.0\\n')\n```\n\nThen providerStatus(config) twice; read file length === 2.\n\nCareful: `providerStatus` iterates `Object.entries(config.providers)` → one pr