{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b6ea-169b-77db-b752-8c7b76b4e97c","session_id":"01a1b6e5-b62d-77fa-b8d6-e09057b99f7b","message":{"id":"01a1b6ea169b77dbb7528c79c16c0a4d","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent. All 11 canaries hit.\n\nLet me double-check it took reasonable time and that canaries really failed (not skipped). Let me verify one manually: run checkRepros and inspect. Actually \"check-defects: ok\" means canary_survived absent for all. Good.\n\nBut let me sanity-verify the a5-forecast-x3 canary truly yields exactly 12 reads and that the repro fails (not a load error). The check passed, and canary requires `not ok ... [repro]`. Good.\n\nNow write test/unit/frozen-files.test.mjs (15 cases) and T-002.json.\n\nDesign of the unit test file. I need helper functions to build mini roots.\n\n```js\nimport test from 'node:test'\nimport assert from 'node:assert/strict'\nimport crypto from 'node:crypto'\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nimport os from 'node:os'\nimport path from 'node:path'\n\nimport { checkFrozenFiles } from '../../scripts/check-frozen-files.mjs'\nimport { checkRepros, reproSha256 } from '../../scripts/check-defects.mjs'\n```\n\nHelpers:\n```js\nconst COPY = 'test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/'\nconst STABLE = 'test/characterization/stable.test.mjs'\n\nfunction hash(text) { return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(text, 'utf8').digest('hex') }\n\nfunction write(root, rel, text) {\n  const file = path.join(root, ...rel.split('/'))\n  fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(file), { recursive: true })\n  fs.writeFileSync(file, text)\n  return file\n}\n\nfunction tempRoot() { return fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'cw-frozen-unit-')) }\n\nfunction baseCopy() {\n  return {\n    [`${COPY}lib/select.mjs`]: \"export const level = 'Quick'\\n\",\n    [`${COPY}config/cliworker.example.json`]: '{\"providers\":{}}\\n',\n    [STABLE]: \"test('[AC-005] stable: placeholder', () => {})\\n\",\n  }\n}\n\nfunction makeRoot(files, registeredPaths) {\n  const root = tempRoot()\n  for (const [rel, text] of Object.entries(files)) write(root, rel, text)\n  const files_ = (registeredPaths ?? Object.keys(files)).map((rel) => ({ path: rel, sha256: hash(files[rel]) }))\n  write(root, 'test/characterization/frozen-files.json', JSON.stringify({ source_commit: 'f75bb76', files: files_ }, null, 2))\n  return root\n}\n```\nCareful: `makeRoot` for tests where a registered file doesn't exist on disk (test 8) — I need to register a path without writing it. I'll make `makeRoot(files, registry)` where registry is a list of `{path, sha256}` explicit. Simpler: pass `files` (written) and `registry` (array of {path, sha256}) defaulting to derived from files.\n\nCases:\n\n1. untouched passes: `checkFrozenFiles({root}).errors` → [].\n2. change one registered byte: use registry computed from files, then rewrite the file with different content → hash_mismatch on select.mjs.\n3. CRLF checkout: register hash of LF text, write file with CRLF → passes.\n4. restricted prefixes: registry adds lib/select.mjs, bin/cliworker.mjs, config/cliworker.example.json with sha '0'*64 → 3 forbidden_path errors.\n5. scripts/ and package.json → outside_allowed.\n6. contracts/spec/x.json + contracts/golden/y.json registered & written → passes (need also copy files registered).\n7. unregistered file in copy → unregistered.\n8. missing registered file → file_missing.\n9. traversal/absolute/backslash → outside_allowed (3 errors).\n\nFor 9, register '../outside.json', 'C:/tmp/x.json', 'lib\\\\select.mjs'. Note 'C:/tmp/x.json' — has drive prefix → outside_allowed. 'lib\\\\select.mjs' → backslash → outside_allowed (not forbidden). Expect 3 errors of outside_allowed. Check details contain the raw strings.\n\nHmm: careful for test 9, if I use `lib\\\\select.mjs`, classify → outside_allowed detail 'lib\\select.mjs'. OK.\n\nNow defects cases 10-15. Mini defect root:\n\n```js\nconst MINI_REPRO = [\n  \"import test from 'node:test'\",\n  \"import assert from 'node:assert/strict'\",\n  '',\n  \"const ANSWER = 1\",\n  '',\n  \"test('[repro] mini defect is present', () => {\",\n  '  assert.equal(ANSWER, 1)',\n  '})',\n  '',\n].join('\\n')\n```\nOpening line ends with `{`, closing `})` at column 0. Good.\n\ndefect ledger entry: `{ id: 'mini', owner_task: 'T-001', owner_milestone: 'M1', ac: 'AC-001', repro_sha256: reproSha256(MINI_REPRO) }`.\n\nRoot layout:\n- test/fixtures/characterization/v0.1/lib/select.mjs (content whatever)\n- test/characterization/defects/mini.test.mjs\n- test/characterization/canary/mini/canary.json + overlay/lib/select.mjs\n\nThen `checkRepros({ root, defects: [{...}] })` → errors [].\n\nCase 11: edit repro (append a line inside block) → repro_changed.\nCase 12: repro恒真 + canary that doesn't affect → canary_survived. That's the same fixture as 10! For case 10 \"well-formed fixture ledger passes\" — but with a 恒真 repro and a no-op canary, the canary run *passes* → canary_survived → not \"passes\". Oops.\n\nSo case 10 needs a repro that passes on copy and fails on canary. Write MINI_REPRO that asserts on a value from the copy:\n\n```js\n\"test('[repro] mini defect is present', async () => {\",\n\"  const mod = await import(pathToFileURL(path.join(process.env.CLIWORKER_V01_ROOT, 'lib', 'select.mjs')).href)\",\n\"  assert.equal(mod.BROKEN, true)\",\n\"})\",\n```\nWith copy lib/select.mjs = `export const BROKEN = true\\n` → passes. Overlay lib/select.mjs = `export const BROKEN = false\\n` → fails.\n\nBut then for case 12 (恒真 repro), use a separate ledger with `assert.ok(true)` repro and overlay/no-op canary.\n\nHmm — case 12's fixture must have a canary that runs (needs overlay or repro_edits). Use an overlay file (unused) so canary run happens and passes → canary_survived.\n\nCase 10 also requires the repro to be a valid [repro] block. Let me define two repro sources: MINI_BROKEN (asserts BROKEN) and MINI_ALWAYS (assert.ok(true)).\n\nFor 10: repro = MINI_BROKEN on copy (BROKEN=true), overlay BROKEN=false.\nFor 12: repro = MINI_ALWAYS